{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Quantity selector
  Phase 8. The three-part control Phase 2 §12.4 specifies: a minus button, a
  number input, a plus button.

  Three consumers, which is why it is a snippet rather than markup inside one
  section: the product form, the cart drawer line, and the cart page line.

  Accepts:
  - id        {String}  required. The input's id. Must be unique on the page.
  - name      {String}  optional, default 'quantity'. Cart lines pass
                        'updates[]', which is the field Shopify's own no-JS
                        cart form uses.
  - value     {Number}  optional, default 1.
  - min       {Number}  optional, default 1.
  - max       {Number}  optional. Omitted unless the variant carries a rule.
  - step      {Number}  optional, default 1.
  - label     {String}  optional, default the 'products.quantity.label' string.
  - describe  {String}  optional. The product or line name, appended to the
                        buttons' accessible names so a screen reader hears
                        which product it is stepping. Phase 2 §12.4 and the
                        Phase 8 brief both ask for this.
  - form      {String}  optional. The id of a form elsewhere in the document,
                        for an input that is not a descendant of it.
  - line_key  {String}  optional. A cart line item key, carried for the cart
                        JavaScript. Never rendered as text.
  - compact   {Boolean} optional, default false. The cart-line size.
  - disabled  {Boolean} optional, default false.

  Usage:
  {% render 'quantity-selector', id: 'Quantity-123', describe: product.title %}

  MINIMUM ONE, ALWAYS.
  The minus button disables itself at the minimum rather than silently
  clamping, so the control never looks live while doing nothing. The input
  still carries min, so a typed 0 or -3 is rejected by the browser and by the
  JavaScript. The Phase 8 brief makes this explicit twice.

  THE UPPER BOUND IS SHOPIFY'S, NOT THE THEME'S.
  max comes from the variant's own quantity rule when the caller passes one.
  Nothing here invents a stock ceiling, and no inventory number is rendered:
  variant.inventory_quantity is public on the storefront and publishing it
  leaks either stock levels or lifetime sales. Phase 1 recorded the inventory
  display question as BUSINESS INFORMATION REQUIRED.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign q_name = name | default: 'quantity'
  assign q_value = value | default: 1
  assign q_min = min | default: 1
  assign q_step = step | default: 1
  assign q_label = label | default: 'products.quantity.label' | t

  assign classes = 'quantity'
  if compact
    assign classes = classes | append: ' quantity--compact'
  endif

  comment
    The decrement is offered only when there is somewhere to go. aria-disabled
    rather than the disabled attribute, per Phase 2 §10.3: the control stays
    focusable so its reason can be reached, and the reason here is the value
    beside it, which already reads as the minimum.
  endcomment
  assign at_min = false
  if q_value <= q_min
    assign at_min = true
  endif
  assign at_max = false
  if max != blank and q_value >= max
    assign at_max = true
  endif
-%}

<div
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-quantity
  {% if line_key != blank %}data-line-key="{{ line_key | escape }}"{% endif %}
>
  <button
    type="button"
    class="quantity__button"
    data-quantity-step="-1"
    {% if at_min or disabled %}aria-disabled="true"{% endif %}
  >
    {% render 'icon-minus' %}
    <span class="visually-hidden">
      {%- if describe != blank -%}
        {{ 'products.quantity.decrease_for' | t: title: describe }}
      {%- else -%}
        {{ 'products.quantity.decrease' | t }}
      {%- endif -%}
    </span>
  </button>

  <label class="visually-hidden" for="{{ id }}">
    {%- if describe != blank -%}
      {{ 'products.quantity.label_for' | t: title: describe }}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ q_label }}
    {%- endif -%}
  </label>
  <input
    class="quantity__input"
    id="{{ id }}"
    type="number"
    name="{{ q_name }}"
    value="{{ q_value }}"
    min="{{ q_min }}"
    step="{{ q_step }}"
    {% if max != blank %}max="{{ max }}"{% endif %}
    inputmode="numeric"
    autocomplete="off"
    {% if form != blank %}form="{{ form }}"{% endif %}
    {% if disabled %}disabled{% endif %}
    data-quantity-input
  >

  <button
    type="button"
    class="quantity__button"
    data-quantity-step="1"
    {% if at_max or disabled %}aria-disabled="true"{% endif %}
  >
    {% render 'icon-plus' %}
    <span class="visually-hidden">
      {%- if describe != blank -%}
        {{ 'products.quantity.increase_for' | t: title: describe }}
      {%- else -%}
        {{ 'products.quantity.increase' | t }}
      {%- endif -%}
    </span>
  </button>
</div>
